---
import LearnArticle from "../../components/LearnArticle.astro";
import ConceptQuiz, { type QuizQuestion } from "../../components/ConceptQuiz.astro";
const QUIZ: QuizQuestion[] = [
	{
		q: "A plane can fly safely on one of its two engines. The second engine is an example of…",
		options: ["Redundancy", "Compression", "Latency", "Abstraction"],
		explain: "Redundancy means extra parts that aren't needed while everything works — there in case something fails.",
	},
	{
		q: "What does it mean for a system to be fault tolerant?",
		options: ["It keeps working even when one of its parts fails", "None of its parts can ever fail", "It shuts down safely whenever something fails", "It has no extra parts"],
		explain: "Fault tolerance is about carrying on despite a failure — usually made possible by redundancy.",
	},
	{
		q: "Computer A can reach computer D along two separate paths. A cable on one path is cut. What happens?",
		options: ["Data still reaches D using the other path", "A and D can no longer communicate", "All data on the network is lost", "The whole network has to be rebuilt"],
		explain: "Having more than one path (redundancy) is what lets the network route around the damage (fault tolerance).",
	},
	{
		q: "What is the main downside of adding redundancy to a system?",
		options: ["It costs more — extra parts, cables, or storage", "It makes the system less reliable", "It stops the system from working normally", "It removes the need for backups"],
		explain: "Every spare costs something. Engineers weigh that cost against how bad a failure would be.",
	},
	{
		q: "Which of these is a single point of failure?",
		options: ["A school where all internet traffic goes through one router", "Photos saved on a phone and also backed up online", "A hospital with backup generators", "A network with many paths between computers"],
		explain: "If that one router breaks, the whole school loses its connection. The other three all have a backup in place.",
	},
];
---

<LearnArticle
	href="/learn/redundancy-and-fault-tolerance"
	description="Redundancy and fault tolerance, explained with spare tires and two-engine planes: how extra parts, copies, and routes keep systems running."
	lead="Why does a car carry a spare tire it almost never uses? The answer is one of the most important ideas in keeping computers running."
>
	<section class="block">
		<p class="block-title">The Spare Tire</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			Most days, the spare tire in a car's trunk does nothing. It's extra weight you carry around
			"just in case." But on the day you get a flat, it's the difference between finishing your trip
			and being stuck at the side of the road.
		</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			Passenger jets work the same way. A plane with two engines is designed and tested so that it
			can keep flying safely on just one. The second engine isn't there because the plane needs both
			— it's there in case one stops.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="block">
		<p class="block-title">Two Words, One Idea</p>
		<div class="compare-grid">
			<div class="compare-card">
				<p class="compare-card-title">Redundancy</p>
				<p>Having <em>extra</em> parts, copies, or paths that aren't needed when everything is working.</p>
				<p>The spare tire. The second engine.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="compare-card compare-card--accent">
				<p class="compare-card-title">Fault tolerance</p>
				<p>The system <em>keeps working</em> even when one of its parts fails.</p>
				<p>You finish the trip. The plane lands safely.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
		<p class="block-callout">
			Redundancy is the cause; fault tolerance is the effect. Adding extra parts (redundancy) is{" "}
			<em>how</em> you build a system that survives a failure (fault tolerance).
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="block block--example">
		<p class="block-title">One Road or Two?</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			Imagine two towns joined by roads. If there's only one road and it floods, the towns are cut
			off. If there are two roads, traffic just takes the other one.
		</p>

		<div class="figure">
			<div class="route-panels">
				<figure class="route-panel">
					<svg viewBox="0 0 220 120" role="img" aria-labelledby="route-one-title">
						<title id="route-one-title">One route between Town A and Town B. It is cut, so the towns are disconnected.</title>
						<line x1="40" y1="60" x2="95" y2="60" class="route-line" />
						<line x1="125" y1="60" x2="180" y2="60" class="route-line" />
						<text x="110" y="67" class="route-cut">✕</text>
						<circle cx="30" cy="60" r="16" class="route-node" />
						<circle cx="190" cy="60" r="16" class="route-node" />
						<text x="30" y="65" class="route-node-label">A</text>
						<text x="190" y="65" class="route-node-label">B</text>
					</svg>
					<figcaption>One route, cut: <strong>disconnected</strong></figcaption>
				</figure>

				<figure class="route-panel">
					<svg viewBox="0 0 220 120" role="img" aria-labelledby="route-two-title">
						<title id="route-two-title">Two routes between Town A and Town B. The bottom route is cut, but the top route still connects them.</title>
						<path d="M42 50 Q110 -5 178 50" class="route-line route-line--active" />
						<path d="M42 70 Q70 100 95 104" class="route-line route-line--dead" />
						<path d="M125 104 Q150 100 178 70" class="route-line route-line--dead" />
						<text x="110" y="111" class="route-cut">✕</text>
						<circle cx="30" cy="60" r="16" class="route-node" />
						<circle cx="190" cy="60" r="16" class="route-node" />
						<text x="30" y="65" class="route-node-label">A</text>
						<text x="190" y="65" class="route-node-label">B</text>
					</svg>
					<figcaption>Two routes, one cut: <strong>still connected</strong></figcaption>
				</figure>
			</div>
		</div>

		<p class="block-text">
			The internet is built like the second picture, only with millions of connections. When a cable
			is damaged or a computer along the way breaks, data simply travels a different path. That's
			why a single broken cable doesn't take the whole internet down — see{" "}
			<a href="/learn/packets-and-routing">Packets &amp; Routing</a> for how data finds its way.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="block">
		<p class="block-title">The Single Point of Failure</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			Engineers hunt for any part that, if it broke, would stop everything. They call it a{" "}
			<em>single point of failure</em>. The one road between the towns is one. So is a school's only
			Wi-Fi router, or a business where only one person knows the password to everything.
		</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			The fix is almost always redundancy: a second road, a backup router, a second person who knows
			how things work.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="block">
		<p class="block-title">Redundancy All Around You</p>
		<ul class="block-list">
			<li>
				<em>Backups.</em> Keeping your photos on your phone <em>and</em> in online storage means losing
				your phone doesn't lose your memories.
			</li>
			<li>
				<em>Data centers.</em> Big websites keep several copies of your data on different computers,
				often in different buildings or even different countries, so a fire or power cut in one place
				doesn't wipe anything out.
			</li>
			<li>
				<em>Backup power.</em> Hospitals have generators that start automatically if the electricity
				goes off.
			</li>
			<li>
				<em>Extra bits.</em> The error-correcting codes on the{" "}
				<a href="/learn/error-correction">Error Correction</a> page add extra bits that aren't part of
				the message itself. Those extra bits are redundancy too — they let the computer notice, and
				even fix, a bit that got flipped by accident.
			</li>
		</ul>
	</section>

	<section class="block">
		<p class="block-title">It Isn't Free</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			Every spare costs something: a spare tire takes up trunk space, a second engine costs money and
			fuel, and every extra copy of data needs somewhere to be stored. So engineers ask how bad a
			failure would be. A lemonade stand might not need a backup table. A hospital definitely needs
			backup power. The more a failure would hurt, the more redundancy is worth paying for.
		</p>
	</section>

	<ConceptQuiz questions={QUIZ} />
</LearnArticle>

<style>
	.route-panels {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 14rem), 1fr));
		gap: 1rem;
	}

	.route-panel {
		margin: 0;
	}

	.route-panel svg {
		display: block;
		width: 100%;
		max-width: 18rem;
		margin: 0 auto;
		height: auto;
	}

	.route-panel figcaption {
		margin-top: 0.5rem;
		font-size: 0.9rem;
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
	}

	.route-panel figcaption strong {
		color: var(--color-text);
	}

	.route-line {
		fill: none;
		stroke: #5b6075;
		stroke-width: 4;
		stroke-dasharray: 6 5;
		stroke-linecap: round;
	}

	.route-line--active {
		stroke: var(--color-accent);
		stroke-dasharray: none;
	}

	.route-cut {
		fill: var(--color-text);
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-size: 20px;
		font-weight: 700;
		text-anchor: middle;
	}

	.route-node {
		fill: var(--color-surface);
		stroke: var(--color-text);
		stroke-width: 2;
	}

	.route-node-label {
		fill: var(--color-text);
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-size: 15px;
		font-weight: 700;
		text-anchor: middle;
	}
</style>
